22%20font-size%3D%2213%22%3EUK%3C%2Ftext%3E%3C%2Fg%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E', 'Car ownership in three countries
The number of cars per 1,000 people in China, Poland and the UK between 1990 and 2020.
Unit: cars per 1,000 people
Series | 1990 | 1995 | 2000 | 2005 | 2010 | 2015 | 2020
China | 2 | 4 | 7 | 20 | 60 | 120 | 190
Poland | 140 | 200 | 260 | 320 | 450 | 580 | 680
UK | 360 | 390 | 420 | 450 | 460 | 470 | 470
Main features: Car ownership rose in all three countries. The UK had the highest level at first, but growth there slowed and levelled off at about 470 cars per 1,000 people. Poland rose fastest, from 140 to 680, overtaking the UK between 2010 and 2015. China started from almost nothing and remained far below the other two, but its growth sped up after 2005, reaching 190 by 2020.', 'Car ownership rose in all three countries. The UK had the highest level at first, but growth there slowed and levelled off at about 470 cars per 1,000 people. Poland rose fastest, from 140 to 680, overtaking the UK between 2010 and 2015. China started from almost nothing and remained far below the other two, but its growth sped up after 2005, reaching 190 by 2020.'),
