Average house prices in London, the South West and the North East of England between 1999 and 2023.
Unit: £ thousands
Series | 1999 | 2003 | 2007 | 2011 | 2015 | 2019 | 2023
London | 180 | 250 | 300 | 330 | 470 | 500 | 520
South West | 110 | 170 | 200 | 195 | 240 | 270 | 310
North East | 70 | 100 | 130 | 125 | 130 | 135 | 160
Main features: Prices rose in all three regions, and London was by far the most expensive throughout, almost tripling from £180,000 to £520,000, with the steepest rise between 2011 and 2015. Prices in the South West and North East dipped slightly after 2007. The North East remained the cheapest region and grew most slowly, although prices still more than doubled to £160,000.', 'Prices rose in all three regions, and London was by far the most expensive throughout, almost tripling from £180,000 to £520,000, with the steepest rise between 2011 and 2015. Prices in the South West and North East dipped slightly after 2007. The North East remained the cheapest region and grew most slowly, although prices still more than doubled to £160,000.'),
