The average number of hours worked per week by employees in four countries between 1970 and 2020.
Unit: hours per week
Series | 1970 | 1980 | 1990 | 2000 | 2010 | 2020
Japan | 44 | 42 | 41 | 38 | 36 | 34
US | 38 | 37 | 37 | 36 | 35 | 35
Germany | 39 | 36 | 34 | 31 | 29 | 27
South Korea | 52 | 53 | 50 | 48 | 43 | 38
Main features: Working hours fell in all four countries. South Korea had the longest working week throughout, although hours fell sharply after 2000, from 48 to 38. Germany had the shortest week from 1980 and saw a steady fall to 27 hours. The US changed least (38 to 35 hours), so that by 2020 Americans worked slightly longer than the Japanese.', 'Working hours fell in all four countries. South Korea had the longest working week throughout, although hours fell sharply after 2000, from 48 to 38. Germany had the shortest week from 1980 and saw a steady fall to 27 hours. The US changed least (38 to 35 hours), so that by 2020 Americans worked slightly longer than the Japanese.'),
