Electricity demand in Britain over 24 hours on a typical winter day and a typical summer day.
Unit: gigawatts
Series | midnight | 3 am | 6 am | 9 am | noon | 3 pm | 6 pm | 9 pm
Winter day | 28 | 24 | 30 | 38 | 37 | 39 | 45 | 38
Summer day | 20 | 17 | 20 | 27 | 28 | 27 | 28 | 25
Main features: Demand was higher in winter at every time of day. Both days had their lowest point at 3 am (24 and 17 gigawatts) and rose sharply in the morning. In winter demand peaked at 45 gigawatts at 6 pm, but in summer it stayed fairly flat at 27-28 gigawatts from 9 am to 6 pm, so the difference between the seasons was greatest in the early evening (17 gigawatts).', 'Demand was higher in winter at every time of day. Both days had their lowest point at 3 am (24 and 17 gigawatts) and rose sharply in the morning. In winter demand peaked at 45 gigawatts at 6 pm, but in summer it stayed fairly flat at 27-28 gigawatts from 9 am to 6 pm, so the difference between the seasons was greatest in the early evening (17 gigawatts).'),
