The sources used to generate electricity in Germany in 2000 and 2023.
2000: Coal 52%, Nuclear 29%, Gas 9%, Renewables 7%, Other 3%
2023: Coal 26%, Nuclear 1%, Gas 15%, Renewables 53%, Other 5%
Main features: In 2000 coal was the main source of electricity (52%), followed by nuclear power (29%), and renewables provided only 7%. By 2023 renewables supplied more than half of all electricity (53%), coal’s share had halved to 26%, and nuclear power had almost disappeared (1%). The share from gas rose from 9% to 15%.', 'In 2000 coal was the main source of electricity (52%), followed by nuclear power (29%), and renewables provided only 7%. By 2023 renewables supplied more than half of all electricity (53%), coal’s share had halved to 26%, and nuclear power had almost disappeared (1%). The share from gas rose from 9% to 15%.'),
