th%3D%22175.0%22%20height%3D%2240%22%20fill%3D%22%23ffffff%22%20stroke%3D%22%2394a3b8%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22467.5%22%20y%3D%22235.0%22%20font-size%3D%2214%22%20font-weight%3D%22normal%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3E8.4%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22555.0%22%20y%3D%22210%22%20width%3D%22175.0%22%20height%3D%2240%22%20fill%3D%22%23ffffff%22%20stroke%3D%22%2394a3b8%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22642.5%22%20y%3D%22235.0%22%20font-size%3D%2214%22%20font-weight%3D%22normal%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3E97%3C%2Ftext%3E%3C%2Fg%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E', 'Screen time, sleep and phones among children
Information about the screen time, sleep and mobile phone ownership of children in four age groups in the UK in 2023.
Age | Screen time per day (hours) | Sleep per night (hours) | Own a mobile phone (%)
5-7 | 1.8 | 10.9 | 4
8-10 | 2.6 | 10.1 | 25
11-13 | 4.1 | 9.2 | 79
14-16 | 5.3 | 8.4 | 97
Main features: Screen time rose steadily with age, almost tripling from 1.8 hours a day for 5-7-year-olds to 5.3 hours for 14-16-year-olds, while sleep fell from 10.9 to 8.4 hours a night. Phone ownership was rare among the youngest children (4%) but jumped from 25% at 8-10 to 79% at 11-13, and almost all 14-16-year-olds had a phone (97%).', 'Screen time rose steadily with age, almost tripling from 1.8 hours a day for 5-7-year-olds to 5.3 hours for 14-16-year-olds, while sleep fell from 10.9 to 8.4 hours a night. Phone ownership was rare among the youngest children (4%) but jumped from 25% at 8-10 to 79% at 11-13, and almost all 14-16-year-olds had a phone (97%).'),
