How electricity is generated from household waste at an energy-from-waste plant.
Stage 1: Lorries deliver household waste
Stage 2: Waste stored in a large bunker
Stage 3: Crane lifts waste into the furnace
Stage 4: Waste burned at 850°C
Stage 5: Heat turns water into steam
Stage 6: Steam drives a turbine to make electricity
Stage 7: Ash removed and used in road building
Stage 8: Gases cleaned before leaving the chimney
Main features: The plant turns rubbish into electricity in a series of stages. Waste is delivered, stored and lifted into a furnace, where it is burned at 850°C. The heat produces steam, which drives a turbine to generate electricity. The process also has two by-products: ash, which is reused in road building, and gases, which are cleaned before release.', 'The plant turns rubbish into electricity in a series of stages. Waste is delivered, stored and lifted into a furnace, where it is burned at 850°C. The heat produces steam, which drives a turbine to generate electricity. The process also has two by-products: ash, which is reused in road building, and gases, which are cleaned before release.'),
